t at the elbow · Criteria A and C exam-style questions", "instr": "Exam-style questions for the lesson \"Antagonistic muscles and movement at the elbow\" (MYP 4, Grade 9 Biology). Criterion A: Knowing and understanding. Criterion C: Processing and evaluating. 13 marks. Do the lesson first. Written answers are marked against the mark scheme and you get detailed feedback; your teacher checks every mark."}, {"id": "2f8487cc-6e9f-5219-8c7b-ba19ffc2ba9b", "title": "Grade 9 · Biology · Body systems · Lesson 15: Movement, exercise and a healthy musculoskeletal system · Criteria A, C and D exam-style questions", "instr": "Exam-style questions for the lesson \"Movement, exercise and a healthy musculoskeletal system\" (MYP 4, Grade 9 Biology). Criterion A: Knowing and understanding. Criterion C: Processing and evaluating. Criterion D: Reflecting on the impacts of science. 12 marks. Do the lesson first. Written answers are marked against the mark scheme and you get detailed feedback; your teacher checks every mark."}, {"id": "74fb4724-9703-5120-9706-6133485a78e2", "title": "Grade 9 · Biology · Body systems · Lesson 16: Homeostasis and negative feedback · Criteria A and D exam-style questions", "instr": "Exam-style questions for the lesson \"Homeostasis and negative feedback\" (MYP 4, Grade 9 Biology). Criterion A: Knowing and understanding. Criterion D: Reflecting on the impacts of science. 12 marks. Do the lesson first. Written answers are marked against the mark scheme and you get detailed feedback; your teacher checks every mark."}, {"id": "b9c8541a-0ae0-5d2e-8302-086d4ee6e2bb", "title": "Grade 9 · Biology · Body systems · Lesson 17: Controlling body temperature · Criteria A and C exam-style questions", "instr": "Exam-style questions for the lesson \"Controlling body temperature\" (MYP 4, Grade 9 Biology). Criterion A: Knowing and understanding. Criterion C: Processing and evaluating. 11 marks. Do the lesson first. Written answers are marked against the mark scheme and you get detailed feedback; your teacher checks every mark."}, {"id": "b494a008-cbfb-5fa9-8550-44fa7a392b25", "title": "Grade 9 · Biology · Body systems · Lesson 18: Controlling blood sugar and water content · Criteria A, C and D exam-style questions", "instr": "Exam-style questions for the lesson \"Controlling blood sugar and water content\" (MYP 4, Grade 9 Biology). Criterion A: Knowing and understanding. Criterion C: Processing and evaluating. Criterion D: Reflecting on the impacts of science. 14 marks. Do the lesson first. Written answers are marked against the mark scheme and you get detailed feedback; your teacher checks every mark."}], "questions": [{"id": "b1aa77f5-99d7-5eeb-bcad-b861cd0e8d60", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 1, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nName the part of the respiratory system.\nAir sac where gas exchange happens", "options": ["Trachea", "Bronchus", "Alveolus", "Diaphragm", "Intercostal muscles"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 2, "model": "Alveolus; diaphragm; trachea; intercostal muscles; bronchus.", "points": null}, {"id": "182653c0-e4a9-5bb4-a3c3-85357d8aa134", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 2, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nName the part of the respiratory system.\nMuscle sheet that separates the chest from the abdomen", "options": ["Trachea", "Bronchus", "Alveolus", "Diaphragm", "Intercostal muscles"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 3, "model": "Alveolus; diaphragm; trachea; intercostal muscles; bronchus.", "points": null}, {"id": "d3836132-61a1-50d2-bab5-43a4ee8fdf08", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 3, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nName the part of the respiratory system.\nTube from the throat to the chest, held open by cartilage", "options": ["Trachea", "Bronchus", "Alveolus", "Diaphragm", "Intercostal muscles"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Alveolus; diaphragm; trachea; intercostal muscles; bronchus.", "points": null}, {"id": "08d81390-5a96-584b-a44e-8efdcb2d7522", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 4, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nName the part of the respiratory system.\nMuscles between the ribs", "options": ["Trachea", "Bronchus", "Alveolus", "Diaphragm", "Intercostal muscles"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 4, "model": "Alveolus; diaphragm; trachea; intercostal muscles; bronchus.", "points": null}, {"id": "726d41f3-2bd4-5937-9fae-49665cba7c3b", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 5, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nName the part of the respiratory system.\nOne of the two tubes that enter the lungs", "options": ["Trachea", "Bronchus", "Alveolus", "Diaphragm", "Intercostal muscles"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "Alveolus; diaphragm; trachea; intercostal muscles; bronchus.", "points": null}, {"id": "f5642874-6130-5e0b-a7b9-0351069c370d", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 6, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nDescribe two ways the nose prepares the air before it reaches the lungs.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "It warms the air [1] and moistens/filters it, with hairs and mucus trapping dust and microbes [1].", "points": [{"text": "It warms the air", "any": ["warm", "heat"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Moistens/filters it, with hairs and mucus trapping dust and microbes", "any": ["moist", "filter", "trap", "hairs", "mucus", "dust"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "330d51d4-a7d3-598f-b393-4ba4350da757", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 7, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nDistinguish between breathing and respiration.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs [1]; respiration is a chemical reaction in cells that releases energy from glucose [1].", "points": [{"text": "Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs", "any": ["air", "in and out", "into and out", "ventilation"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Respiration is a chemical reaction in cells that releases energy from glucose", "any": ["reaction", "release energy", "releases energy", "glucose", "cells"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "7bf665d6-14c2-50c8-867e-20b903917f5b", "quiz": "5353ad28-9a97-5384-baf2-f42e321e355d", "pos": 8, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion D: Reflecting on the impacts of science]\nSuggest one reason why a person who lives in a city with polluted air may have more breathing problems than a person in a rural village.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion D", "correct": null, "model": "City air contains more dust, smoke and exhaust gases [1] that irritate and damage the airways, causing inflammation, extra mucus or asthma [1].", "points": [{"text": "City air contains more dust, smoke and exhaust gases", "any": ["dust", "smoke", "particles", "exhaust", "pollut", "gases"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "That irritate and damage the airways, causing inflammation, extra mucus or asthma", "any": ["irritat", "damage", "inflam", "mucus", "asthma", "block", "narrow", "infection"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "f1ba2e9f-8a35-5d96-b198-7f8aa0c226c7", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 1, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nDefine diffusion.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "The net movement of particles [1] from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration [1].", "points": [{"text": "The net movement of particles", "any": ["net movement", "movement", "spread"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "From a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration", "any": ["high", "higher", "low", "lower", "concentration"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "4fa8ab19-689d-5877-aa7a-da9f6d0f5e35", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 2, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nTrue or false?\nOxygen diffuses from the blood into the alveoli", "options": ["True", "False"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "Oxygen enters the blood; walls are one cell thick; CO₂ leaves the blood; fewer, larger sacs reduce the area.", "points": null}, {"id": "8809530e-33af-56d1-a2f0-d5137bf74f54", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 3, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nTrue or false?\nThe wall of an alveolus is one cell thick", "options": ["True", "False"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Oxygen enters the blood; walls are one cell thick; CO₂ leaves the blood; fewer, larger sacs reduce the area.", "points": null}, {"id": "42e4cdfe-3ea5-55a5-8342-cd14695da483", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 4, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nTrue or false?\nCarbon dioxide diffuses into the alveoli to be breathed out", "options": ["True", "False"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Oxygen enters the blood; walls are one cell thick; CO₂ leaves the blood; fewer, larger sacs reduce the area.", "points": null}, {"id": "7fabc8c3-6797-5efb-891a-6906b9d9b53d", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 5, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nTrue or false?\nFewer, larger alveoli give a bigger surface area", "options": ["True", "False"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "Oxygen enters the blood; walls are one cell thick; CO₂ leaves the blood; fewer, larger sacs reduce the area.", "points": null}, {"id": "57199125-bab9-51af-bfbb-17559d82c48c", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 6, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nExplain how three features of the alveoli make them efficient at gas exchange.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "A large surface area lets more gas diffuse at once [1]; thin walls one cell thick give a short diffusion distance [1]; a rich capillary network keeps the concentration gradient steep [1].", "points": [{"text": "A large surface area lets more gas diffuse at once", "any": ["surface area", "large area", "many alveoli"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Thin walls one cell thick give a short diffusion distance", "any": ["thin", "one cell", "short distance"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "A rich capillary network keeps the concentration gradient steep", "any": ["capillar", "blood supply", "moist", "ventilat", "gradient"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "7aacb5bd-0a4a-51b8-822e-97fd35351287", "quiz": "e4b12f2c-94f3-5f6c-adca-5dee3c588a3b", "pos": 7, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion C: Processing and evaluating]\nCube A has sides of 1 cm and cube B has sides of 3 cm. Calculate the surface area to volume ratio of each and state which would gain oxygen by diffusion more effectively.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion C", "correct": null, "model": "Cube A: 6 cm² ÷ 1 cm³ = 6 : 1 [1]; cube B: 54 cm² ÷ 27 cm³ = 2 : 1 [1]; cube A, the smaller cube, has the larger ratio so it gains oxygen more effectively [1].", "points": [{"text": "Cube A: 6 cm² ÷ 1 cm³ = 6 : 1", "any": ["6 : 1", "6:1", "6/1", "ratio of 6", "6 to 1", "= 6"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Cube B: 54 cm² ÷ 27 cm³ = 2 : 1", "any": ["2 : 1", "2:1", "54/27", "54 ÷ 27", "= 2", "2 to 1"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Cube A, the smaller cube, has the larger ratio so it gains oxygen more effectively", "any": ["cube a", "a ", "smaller", "1 cm"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "93e1855f-316a-5fb2-8f19-4b2fa048d3cc", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 1, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nInhalation or exhalation (at rest)?\nThe diaphragm contracts and flattens", "options": ["Inhalation", "Exhalation"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Diaphragm contracts and pressure falls when breathing in; ribs fall and volume decreases when breathing out.", "points": null}, {"id": "c9fb8a65-6da5-5fe5-ba65-ebefbf4f2598", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 2, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nInhalation or exhalation (at rest)?\nThe ribs move down and in", "options": ["Inhalation", "Exhalation"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "Diaphragm contracts and pressure falls when breathing in; ribs fall and volume decreases when breathing out.", "points": null}, {"id": "967b74b2-10b4-5fa0-8e99-7c4d74877384", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 3, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nInhalation or exhalation (at rest)?\nThe pressure in the lungs is lower than the air outside", "options": ["Inhalation", "Exhalation"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Diaphragm contracts and pressure falls when breathing in; ribs fall and volume decreases when breathing out.", "points": null}, {"id": "fb9fcdd1-bcbb-5816-8047-00566881ea6d", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 4, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nInhalation or exhalation (at rest)?\nThe volume of the thorax decreases", "options": ["Inhalation", "Exhalation"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "Diaphragm contracts and pressure falls when breathing in; ribs fall and volume decreases when breathing out.", "points": null}, {"id": "586c8b59-8560-5f41-8940-c14210c73cef", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 5, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nOutline the process of exhalation at rest.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "The diaphragm relaxes and returns to a dome [1]; the ribs move down and in so the volume of the thorax decreases [1]; the pressure increases and air flows out [1].", "points": [{"text": "The diaphragm relaxes and returns to a dome", "any": ["diaphragm relaxes", "dome", "diaphragm moves up"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The ribs move down and in so the volume of the thorax decreases", "any": ["volume decreases", "volume of the thorax decreases", "smaller volume", "ribs down", "ribs move down"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The pressure increases and air flows out", "any": ["pressure increases", "higher pressure", "pressure rises", "air out", "air flows out", "air is pushed out"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "9315c793-8109-58f9-aab5-4ac259f1bb7f", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 6, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nExplain why the lungs need the diaphragm and the intercostal muscles to fill with air.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "The lungs have no muscles of their own [1]; the diaphragm and intercostal muscles change the volume of the chest and so the pressure, which makes air flow in [1].", "points": [{"text": "The lungs have no muscles of their own", "any": ["no muscle", "cannot move", "do not have muscle", "can't move"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The diaphragm and intercostal muscles change the volume of the chest and so the pressure, which makes air flow in", "any": ["volume", "pressure", "change the size"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "6d8ea079-8896-5897-ac59-ed52d26ce775", "quiz": "8fea4ef4-f2cc-5cfb-a782-916e616e94da", "pos": 7, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion C: Processing and evaluating]\nA student counts 18 breaths in 60 s at rest and 12 breaths in 20 s after exercise. Calculate the breathing rate after exercise and the percentage increase.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion C", "correct": null, "model": "After exercise: 12 × 3 = 36 breaths per minute [1]; increase = 36 − 18 = 18, and 18 ÷ 18 × 100 [1] = 100% increase [1].", "points": [{"text": "After exercise: 12 × 3 = 36 breaths per minute", "any": ["36"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Increase = 36 − 18 = 18, and 18 ÷ 18 × 100", "any": ["18 ÷ 18", "18/18", "increase of 18", "36 - 18", "36 − 18", "difference"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "= 100% increase", "any": ["100"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "74041e64-dd82-5e79-a2d6-ea1dad71d5f6", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 1, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nComplete the table: inhaled or exhaled air?\n21% oxygen", "options": ["Inhaled air", "Exhaled air"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "21% oxygen and 0.04% CO₂: inhaled. 4% CO₂ and saturated with water: exhaled.", "points": null}, {"id": "9a3dce57-1daa-5b7c-9282-335eb0ab7b23", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 2, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nComplete the table: inhaled or exhaled air?\n4% carbon dioxide", "options": ["Inhaled air", "Exhaled air"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "21% oxygen and 0.04% CO₂: inhaled. 4% CO₂ and saturated with water: exhaled.", "points": null}, {"id": "96c5ab8a-8512-5b08-9c9d-7c49bccee09f", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 3, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nComplete the table: inhaled or exhaled air?\nSaturated with water vapour", "options": ["Inhaled air", "Exhaled air"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 1, "model": "21% oxygen and 0.04% CO₂: inhaled. 4% CO₂ and saturated with water: exhaled.", "points": null}, {"id": "e07108aa-3306-580c-8dea-8c39746b6119", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 4, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nComplete the table: inhaled or exhaled air?\n0.04% carbon dioxide", "options": ["Inhaled air", "Exhaled air"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "21% oxygen and 0.04% CO₂: inhaled. 4% CO₂ and saturated with water: exhaled.", "points": null}, {"id": "a12f91df-2e5d-57d4-a9f7-5738197051d0", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 5, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nExplain why exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide than inhaled air.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "Carbon dioxide is produced by respiration in the cells [1]; it is carried by the blood to the lungs, where it diffuses into the alveoli and is breathed out [1].", "points": [{"text": "Carbon dioxide is produced by respiration in the cells", "any": ["respiration", "cells produce", "made in cells", "produced by cells"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "It is carried by the blood to the lungs, where it diffuses into the alveoli and is breathed out", "any": ["diffuses", "into the alveoli", "out of the blood", "breathed out"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "354dccee-7036-5073-ba70-727fa7a8c302", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 6, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion B: Inquiring and designing]\nDesign an experiment to compare the carbon dioxide in a student's exhaled air before and after exercise. State the independent and dependent variables and two control variables.", "options": [], "marks": 4, "section": "Criterion B", "correct": null, "model": "Independent: before or after exercise [1]; dependent: time for the limewater (or indicator) to change colour / turn milky [1]; controls: the same volume and concentration of limewater [1] and the same number of breaths from the same person [1].", "points": [{"text": "Independent: before or after exercise", "any": ["before and after", "exercise"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Dependent: time for the limewater (or indicator) to change colour / turn milky", "any": ["milky", "time", "colour", "indicator", "cloudy"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Controls: the same volume and concentration of limewater", "any": ["volume", "same amount of limewater", "concentration"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The same number of breaths from the same person", "any": ["number of breaths", "same breath", "same person", "same time", "temperature"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "e90ff6ab-6769-5821-a4fb-df79f91bf4a6", "quiz": "8d14ec7d-45b8-5514-b13c-88b806dcf174", "pos": 7, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion C: Processing and evaluating]\nExhaled air at rest has 16% oxygen; after exercise it has 14%. Inhaled air has 21%. Calculate the oxygen absorbed in each case and explain the difference.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion C", "correct": null, "model": "At rest 21 − 16 = 5% is absorbed [1]; after exercise 21 − 14 = 7% [1]; more oxygen is absorbed because the muscles respire faster to release more energy [1].", "points": [{"text": "At rest 21 − 16 = 5% is absorbed", "any": ["5"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "After exercise 21 − 14 = 7%", "any": ["7"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "More oxygen is absorbed because the muscles respire faster to release more energy", "any": ["respire", "respiration", "muscles", "more oxygen", "energy"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "8e1f3609-4ccd-50c7-9684-6ababed6e150", "quiz": "f174965b-3eac-5bc7-b35d-f03e849f5c32", "pos": 1, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nDefine tidal volume and vital capacity.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "Tidal volume: the volume of air breathed in or out in one normal breath at rest [1]; vital capacity: the largest volume of air that can be breathed out after the deepest possible breath in [1].", "points": [{"text": "Tidal volume: the volume of air breathed in or out in one normal breath at rest", "any": ["normal breath", "one breath", "at rest"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Vital capacity: the largest volume of air that can be breathed out after the deepest possible breath in", "any": ["largest", "maximum", "deepest", "biggest"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "ad8f966b-bfa9-5154-8c1e-3b6a3de10d6d", "quiz": "f174965b-3eac-5bc7-b35d-f03e849f5c32", "pos": 2, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion C: Processing and evaluating]\nA girl has a tidal volume of 0.4 dm³ and a breathing rate of 16 per minute at rest. During exercise these become 1.6 dm³ and 32 per minute. Calculate the minute volume in each case.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion C", "correct": null, "model": "At rest: 0.4 × 16 = 6.4 dm³ per minute [1]; during exercise: 1.6 × 32 [1] = 51.2 dm³ per minute [1].", "points": [{"text": "At rest: 0.4 × 16 = 6.4 dm³ per minute", "any": ["6.4"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "During exercise: 1.6 × 32", "any": ["1.6 × 32", "1.6 x 32"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "= 51.2 dm³ per minute", "any": ["51.2"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "b6cb1c45-1178-514c-a996-55aa963741e9", "quiz": "f174965b-3eac-5bc7-b35d-f03e849f5c32", "pos": 3, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nExplain what is meant by an oxygen debt.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "During hard exercise muscles respire anaerobically and produce lactic acid [1]; the extra oxygen needed after exercise to break down the lactic acid is the oxygen debt [1].", "points": [{"text": "During hard exercise muscles respire anaerobically and produce lactic acid", "any": ["anaerobic", "lactic acid"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The extra oxygen needed after exercise to break down the lactic acid is the oxygen debt", "any": ["oxygen", "break down", "remove", "after exercise"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "b6df9c3f-d93f-5b09-b23b-670879476989", "quiz": "f174965b-3eac-5bc7-b35d-f03e849f5c32", "pos": 4, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion B: Inquiring and designing]\nSuggest a testable hypothesis about whether trained runners have a larger vital capacity than non-runners, and state how you would make the comparison fair.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion B", "correct": null, "model": "If students run regularly, then their vital capacity will be larger than that of non-runners [1], because training strengthens the diaphragm and intercostal muscles [1]; compare students of the same age, sex and height, using the same spirometer and the mean of three readings [1].", "points": [{"text": "If students run regularly, then their vital capacity will be larger than that of non-runners", "any": ["if", "runners", "larger"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Because training strengthens the diaphragm and intercostal muscles", "any": ["because", "stronger", "muscles", "diaphragm", "training"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Compare students of the same age, sex and height, using the same spirometer and the mean of three readings", "any": ["same age", "same height", "same sex", "same spirometer", "repeat", "mean", "same gender"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "3d2cccc4-6f0f-52c6-8f41-6179910e85c4", "quiz": "d2ef1e3e-e1d2-56bf-a8a1-2c7d19de7bd1", "pos": 1, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nDescribe how mucus and cilia work together to protect the lungs.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "Mucus traps dust and microbes [1]; cilia beat to sweep the mucus up to the throat, away from the lungs [1].", "points": [{"text": "Mucus traps dust and microbes", "any": ["trap", "sticky", "catch"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Cilia beat to sweep the mucus up to the throat, away from the lungs", "any": ["sweep", "beat", "throat", "upward", "move the mucus"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "d0d55ec7-8e75-5c1d-a3e6-5dd8ad7b317e", "quiz": "d2ef1e3e-e1d2-56bf-a8a1-2c7d19de7bd1", "pos": 2, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nCarbon monoxide in smoke combines with haemoglobin. Explain why this makes a smoker breathless during exercise.", "options": [], "marks": 2, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": null, "model": "Haemoglobin combined with carbon monoxide cannot carry oxygen, so the blood carries less oxygen [1]; the muscles get less oxygen for respiration, so the smoker breathes faster and becomes breathless [1].", "points": [{"text": "Haemoglobin combined with carbon monoxide cannot carry oxygen, so the blood carries less oxygen", "any": ["less oxygen", "cannot carry oxygen", "reduces oxygen", "oxygen carried"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "The muscles get less oxygen for respiration, so the smoker breathes faster and becomes breathless", "any": ["respiration", "muscles", "energy", "breathe faster", "breathless"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "b45561fb-52da-549b-ac16-228d5764d3d4", "quiz": "d2ef1e3e-e1d2-56bf-a8a1-2c7d19de7bd1", "pos": 3, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion C: Processing and evaluating]\nIn a study, 4 out of 1000 non-smokers and 56 out of 1000 heavy smokers developed lung cancer. Calculate the percentage in each group and how many times greater the risk is for smokers.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion C", "correct": null, "model": "Non-smokers: 4 ÷ 1000 × 100 = 0.4% [1]; heavy smokers: 56 ÷ 1000 × 100 = 5.6% [1]; 5.6 ÷ 0.4 = 14 times greater [1].", "points": [{"text": "Non-smokers: 4 ÷ 1000 × 100 = 0.4%", "any": ["0.4"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Heavy smokers: 56 ÷ 1000 × 100 = 5.6%", "any": ["5.6"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "5.6 ÷ 0.4 = 14 times greater", "any": ["14"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "27aa3ced-3e74-54b1-bd06-22186c4fd4e9", "quiz": "d2ef1e3e-e1d2-56bf-a8a1-2c7d19de7bd1", "pos": 4, "kind": "short", "prompt": "[Criterion D: Reflecting on the impacts of science]\nDiscuss whether the government should ban smoking in all public places. Give one argument for, one against and a conclusion.", "options": [], "marks": 3, "section": "Criterion D", "correct": null, "model": "For: it protects non-smokers and children from second-hand smoke and its diseases [1]; against: it limits people's freedom of choice and is hard to enforce, and some businesses may lose income [1]; overall I think it should be banned because the health of others matters more than one person's choice [1].", "points": [{"text": "For: it protects non-smokers and children from second-hand smoke and its diseases", "any": ["passive", "second-hand", "others", "children", "health", "protect", "non-smokers"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Against: it limits people's freedom of choice and is hard to enforce, and some businesses may lose income", "any": ["freedom", "choice", "rights", "business", "jobs", "enforce", "hard to", "income", "tax"], "all": [], "marks": 1}, {"text": "Overall I think it should be banned because the health of others matters more than one person's choice", "any": ["i think", "overall", "conclusion", "should", "because"], "all": [], "marks": 1}]}, {"id": "8b0682ca-f0ed-51f4-bf41-d7dc7c17ab09", "quiz": "e943634d-9fe6-514e-9002-fa5f24d890d1", "pos": 1, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nMatch each term to its definition.\nA change in the environment that can be detected", "options": ["Stimulus", "Receptor", "Effector", "Coordinator"], "marks": 1, "section": "Criterion A", "correct": 0, "model": "Stimulus; receptor; effector; coordinator.", "points": null}, {"id": "12838964-9eba-5e1a-bd33-879842981dad", "quiz": "e943634d-9fe6-514e-9002-fa5f24d890d1", "pos": 2, "kind": "mcq", "prompt": "[Criterion A: Knowing and understanding]\nMatch each term to its defini